prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages the review and execution of commercial leases at a real-estate brokerage.

The people involved are leasing agents, transaction coordinators, brokers, compliance officers, and auditors. One person may hold both the leasing agent and transaction coordinator roles at the same time, and one person may hold both the broker and compliance officer roles at the same time.

The resources are leases and executed leases, where every lease records its listing agent, the broker who reviewed it, its lease code, its monthly rent, its term in months, its square footage, its region, its premises class, its contingencies, its market segment, whether it is under a fair-housing hold, its status, and the close of its signing window.

Each request carries the current time, whether it comes from the brokerage network, whether the counterparty's identity has been verified, and whether a court order has been declared.

A leasing agent may draft a lease, moving it into the drafted state, and an auditor may view the audit log.

The listing agent who drafted a lease may not be the person who reviews it, and someone who did not draft the lease is free to review it.

This independence requirement applies only when the total contract value, the monthly rent multiplied by the term in months, crosses the high-value threshold, and when the total contract value stays at or below that threshold the requirement does not bind.

The independence requirement is relaxed when the leased square footage crosses the large-premises threshold, so on a large-premises lease the person who listed it may also review it; on a smaller lease the person who listed it may not review it.

A lease may be reviewed only by someone who holds the broker role required for licensed review, and a reviewer who does not hold that broker role may not review it.

When a court order has been declared, a broker may review a lease they themselves listed, setting aside the separation between listing and reviewing; without a declared court order, that separation stands.

If these two rules disagree on a single review, the court-order directive wins over the separation between listing and reviewing.

Absent both a declared court order and a large-premises footprint, neither a broker nor a transaction coordinator who listed a high-value lease may review it, and no one lacking the required broker role may review it.

A request by a licensed broker to review a high-value lease that they did not draft, tripping no prohibition above, must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity LeasingAgent {
    user: User,
};

entity TransactionCoordinator {
    user: User,
};

entity Broker {
    user: User,
};

entity ComplianceOfficer {
    user: User,
};

entity Auditor {
    user: User,
};

entity User in [LeasingAgent, TransactionCoordinator, Broker, ComplianceOfficer, Auditor] {
    holdsBrokerRole: Bool,
    holdsLeasingAgentRole: Bool,
    holdsAuditorRole: Bool,
};

entity Lease {
    listingAgent: User,
    reviewedBy?: User,
    leaseCode: String,
    monthlyRent: Long,
    termInMonths: Long,
    squareFootage: Long,
    region: String,
    premisesClass: String,
    contingencies: Set<String>,
    marketSegment: String,
    fairHousingHold: Bool,
    status: String,
    signingWindowClosesAt: datetime,
    draftedBy: User,
    isHighValue: Bool,
    hasDeclaredCourtOrder: Bool,
    hasLargePremisesFootprint: Bool,
};

entity ExecutedLease;

entity AuditLog;

action draftLease appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Lease],
    context: {
        currentTime: datetime,
        fromBrokerageNetwork: Bool,
        counterpartyIdentityVerified: Bool,
        courtOrderDeclared: Bool,
    },
};

action reviewLease appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Lease],
    context: {
        currentTime: datetime,
        fromBrokerageNetwork: Bool,
        counterpartyIdentityVerified: Bool,
        courtOrderDeclared: Bool,
        highValueThreshold: Long,
        largePremisesThreshold: Long,
    },
};

action viewAuditLog appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [AuditLog],
    context: {
        currentTime: datetime,
        fromBrokerageNetwork: Bool,
        counterpartyIdentityVerified: Bool,
        courtOrderDeclared: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
